  "answer": "No. The Aussie is relatively strong against the yen right now, though it has slipped a little from its recent peak.\n\n- **Latest rate:** A$1 bought ¥109.67 on 16 October 2026. It has stayed between about ¥109.4 and ¥110.7 so far this month.\n- **Over the past year:** It was ¥100.84 in October 2025, so the Aussie is up about 8.8% against the yen. It climbed to a high of ¥114.28 in April 2026 and ¥114.54 in August.\n- **Recent dip:** It has fallen about 4% from the August level of ¥114.54. At the end of September it was ¥109.47.\n- **Against other currencies:** The trade-weighted index was 64.4 in October 2026, up from 61.2 a year earlier. It is slightly below its May 2026 high of 66.6.\n\nThe Aussie is well above its level of a year ago against the yen, but a little below its 2026 highs. Whether that counts as weak depends on your reference point. For example, it would look different if you compared it with levels from several years ago, and I only pulled data back to October 2025.",
